{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 45, :comments ["<b>Tzaphenat Pa'neach.</b> Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] (on Genesis 41:45) said: if it is an Egyptian word, we do not know its meaning, and if it is translated [from Hebrew], we do not know Joseph's [original] name. According to the opinion of the earlier ones who say it means \"the revealer of hidden things,\" it is possible that he gave him an honorable name in the language of his [Pharaoh's] own land, for he asked him [for a Hebrew name], or the king knew the language of the land of Canaan, which was close to him, and its meaning is \"revealer of that which is hidden.\" And similarly, his [Pharaoh's] daughter called our master Moses by a name in the language of her people: \"from the water I drew him\" (Exodus 2:10). And do not be astonished that the scribes of the Egyptians called him Monius, for they change names into a language that is understood or familiar to them, as the Targum does with some of them — for example, \"between Kadesh and Shur\" (Genesis 20:1) — \"between Rekam and between Chagra,\" and so with many names; and some of them they do not change at all, as he did with Sichon king of Cheshbon and Og king of the Bashan and many others besides. This is according to what they were called in the Aramaic tongue in his [the Targumist's] generation. And the Christian translators did likewise." "<b>The daughter of Potiphera, priest of On.</b> This is Potiphar, and he was called Potiphera because he castrated himself [נסתרס] since [his daughter] took Joseph for a wife — this is the language of Rashi (on Genesis 41:45), and it is from the midrash of our Sages (Sotah 13b). Rashi (on Genesis 47:22) was forced by this to say that every כֹּהֵן means a minister of deity, except for this one, which means a great officer, since he was the chief executioner, and likewise כֹּהֵן מִדְיָן (Exodus 2:16). But I say that according to the words of our Sages, Potiphar had been an officer of Pharaoh, and when he castrated himself and people recognized it in him and called him Potiphera, he was ashamed of the matter and withdrew from his office and entered himself into a house of idol worship and became a priest of idolatry, for such is the custom of the nobles. And it is possible that On was the name of his idolatry, and likewise כֹּהֵן מִדְיָן means a [pagan] priest, as they said (Sanhedrin 82a) about Jethro, that he would fatten calves for idolatry. And the truth is that the term kehunah [priesthood] means service, but not service of deity alone, for behold, \"and the sons of David were kohanim\" (II Samuel 8:18), and in its place in Chronicles (I Chronicles 18:17) [it says], \"and the sons of David were the first at the hand of the king,\" and likewise, \"He leads priests away stripped\" (Job 12:19) — they are the ministers of the king. And likewise, \"to minister [יכהן] in beauty\" (Isaiah 61:10): he shall clothe himself in splendor like a serving priest — they shall make him garments unlike those of the rest of the people, for honor and glory, and likewise, \"and his acquaintances and his kohanim\" (II Kings 10:11): the chiefs of his attendants."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Ibn Ezra and expands; 'Monius' is Josephus's Greek form of Joseph's Egyptian name (Manetho/Josephus tradition), transliterated here in Hebrew.", 1 "The phrase נסתרס is rendered literally as 'castrated himself,' following Rashi on this verse citing Sotah 13b; the argument then turns on whether כהן always means a priest of deity or can mean a civil officer/minister."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:26.442643227Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}